<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="types-gapfill">
  <title>Fill in the Gaps</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      PreTeXt has no CodeRunner-style gap-filler, where an editor has locked
      code with editable holes in it. The options below each give up something
      different.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-gap-activecode">
    <title>ActiveCode with blanks and hidden tests</title>
    <p>
      The student overwrites the <c>____</c> markers in a normal editor.
      Hidden unit tests check behaviour, so any correct completion passes.
      The rest of the code is <em>not</em> locked, though.
    </p>
    <exercise label="gap-ac-findmin">
      <title>Complete <c>find_min</c></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Replace each <c>____</c> so that <c>find_min</c> returns the
        smallest number. Change nothing else. Then press <em>Run</em>
        to run the hidden tests.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="ac-gap-findmin">
        <code><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        if ____:
            best = ____
    return best
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(find_min([8, 3, 9, 1, 6]), 1, "find_min([8, 3, 9, 1, 6])")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([4, 2, 7, 2, 9]), 2, "find_min([4, 2, 7, 2, 9])")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([5]), 5, "a single element")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([-3, -8, -1]), -8, "negative numbers")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-gap-locked">
    <title>ActiveCode with locked code around one gap</title>
    <p>
      A visible <c>preamble</c> and <c>postamble</c> are shown but cannot be
      edited, so only the middle region can change. This works when the
      gap is one <em>contiguous</em> region. Two separate gaps need two
      exercises, or the next technique.
    </p>
    <exercise label="gap-locked-cond">
      <title>Only the loop body is yours</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Write the two lines of the loop body: compare, then maybe overwrite.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="ac-gap-locked">
        <preamble visible="yes"><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
]]></preamble>
        <code><![CDATA[
        # your two lines here (keep the 8-space indent)
]]></code>
        <postamble visible="yes"><![CDATA[
    return best
]]></postamble>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(find_min([8, 3, 9, 1, 6]), 1, "find_min([8, 3, 9, 1, 6])")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([5, 3, 8, 9]), 3, "find_min([5, 3, 8, 9])")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([-3, -8, -1]), -8, "negative numbers")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-gap-fitb">
    <title>Fill-in-the-blank with numbered gaps</title>
    <p>
      The code is shown with numbered gaps. The student types into one blank
      per gap, and each blank is checked with a regular expression that
      tolerates spacing and the equivalent forms you list. The code itself
      is never run, so the regexes must cover every acceptable answer.
    </p>
    <exercise label="gap-fitb-findmin">
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        if ___1___:
            best = ___2___
    return best
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>Gap 1: <c>if</c> <fillin mode="string" characters="20"/> <c>:</c></p>
        <p>Gap 2: <c>best =</c> <fillin mode="string" characters="20"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>(numbers\[\s*i\s*\]\s*&lt;\s*best|best\s*&gt;\s*numbers\[\s*i\s*\])</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*&gt;.*</strcmp><feedback><p>That keeps the <em>largest</em> value seen so far.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*&lt;=.*</strcmp><feedback><p>That also works, but it overwrites on ties. Use strict <c>&lt;</c> to keep the first.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>numbers\[\s*i\s*\]</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>i</strcmp><feedback><p><c>i</c> is the position. <c>best</c> holds a value.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-gap-inline">
    <title>Fill-in-the-blank with the blanks inside the code</title>
    <p>
      The same idea as the previous technique, but the blanks sit
      <em>where they belong</em> in the code, so the student never has to
      match <q>gap 1</q> to a position. PreTeXt does not allow a
      <c>fillin</c> inside a program listing (code lines are plain text), so
      the code is laid out as a borderless table with one row per line:
      code fragments in <c>c</c> elements, blanks between them, and
      indentation written with non-breaking spaces, which keep their width
      in the code font. Grading is still by regular expression per blank;
      nothing is run.
    </p>
    <exercise label="gap-fitb-inline">
      <title>Complete <c>find_min</c> in place</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Fill in the three blanks so that <c>find_min</c> returns the smallest number in a non-empty list.</p>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row><cell><c>def find_min(numbers):</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>    best = </c><fillin mode="string" characters="10"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>        if </c><fillin mode="string" characters="18"/><c>:</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>            best = </c><fillin mode="string" characters="10"/></cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>    return best</c></cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>numbers\[\s*0\s*\]</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>0</strcmp><feedback><p>A seed of 0 fails when every number is positive. Start from a real element of the list.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>numbers</strcmp><feedback><p><c>numbers</c> is the whole list. <c>best</c> must hold one number.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>(numbers\[\s*i\s*\]\s*&lt;\s*best|best\s*&gt;\s*numbers\[\s*i\s*\])</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*&gt;.*</strcmp><feedback><p>That keeps the <em>largest</em> value seen so far.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*&lt;=.*</strcmp><feedback><p>That also works, but it overwrites on ties. Use strict <c>&lt;</c>.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>numbers\[\s*i\s*\]</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>i</strcmp><feedback><p><c>i</c> is the position. <c>best</c> holds a value.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      <em>Trade-offs:</em> this reads like code and supports any number of
      blanks, with feedback aimed at specific misconceptions. But the code is
      not runnable, there is no syntax highlighting, copying it gives
      non-breaking spaces, and the regexes must list every acceptable form.
      Writing the table by hand is tedious, so for a whole course you would
      generate it from ordinary code with <c>____</c> markers by a small
      script.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-gap-parsons">
    <title>Parsons problem with paired distractors</title>
    <p>
      The student puts lines in order and picks the right version where two
      are offered. This is gap-filling by <em>recognition</em>. It works on
      phones and needs no typing or code execution, which suits weak
      connections and early sessions.
    </p>
    <exercise label="gap-parsons-findmin" language="python" indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>Build <c>find_min</c>. Where two versions of a line are offered, pick the correct one.</p>
      </statement>
      <blocks>
        <block><cline>def find_min(numbers):</cline></block>
        <block>
          <choice correct="yes"><cline>    best = numbers[0]</cline></choice>
          <choice><cline>    best = 0</cline></choice>
        </block>
        <block><cline>    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):</cline></block>
        <block>
          <choice correct="yes"><cline>        if numbers[i] &lt; best:</cline></choice>
          <choice><cline>        if numbers[i] &gt; best:</cline></choice>
        </block>
        <block><cline>            best = numbers[i]</cline></block>
        <block><cline>    return best</cline></block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-gap-hparsons">
    <title>Horizontal Parsons: assemble one line from tokens</title>
    <p>
      A <q>micro-Parsons</q> problem, where the student builds a single line
      from token blocks and unused distractor tokens are allowed. Good for
      one gap whose difficulty is in getting the syntax right.
    </p>
    <exercise label="gap-hparsons-cond" language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>Build the condition line of <c>find_min</c>.</p>
      </statement>
      <blocks layout="horizontal" randomize="yes">
        <block><c>if</c></block>
        <block><c>numbers[i]</c></block>
        <block><c>&lt;</c></block>
        <block><c>best</c></block>
        <block><c>:</c></block>
        <block correct="no"><c>&gt;</c></block>
        <block correct="no"><c>i</c></block>
        <block correct="no"><c>=</c></block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
